# Cloudflare Pages reads this file and applies it before the static handler.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. A post was published in Portuguese on 2026-09-03 and republished
# in English the same day, which changed its slug. The generator wipes blog/ on
# every run, so the old page left every build at once. The old URL then answered
# 200 with the complete styled article for hours, across six production builds.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED, and it is worth keeping because the mechanism is not settled.
# On the apex the orphan carried:
#
#   Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=604800
#   Age: 2402, climbing one second per second across polls
#   x-robots-tag: noindex
#
# Every live HTML page on the same apex carries max-age=0, must-revalidate, and no
# Age and no robots header at all. Nothing in this repository emits s-maxage or
# noindex; public/_headers sets Cache-Control for /assets/* alone. Meanwhile the
# three production builds either side of the incident all answer a hard 404 at that
# path on their own *.pages.dev URLs, so the origin genuinely had nothing there
# while the apex served a styled 200.
#
# THE MECHANISM IS NOT NAMED HERE, ON PURPOSE. The first reading was Cloudflare
# Always Online, and Cloudflare's own documentation contradicts it: Always Online
# serves an archive when Cloudflare cannot reach the origin at all, which is a 520
# to 527, and a 404 is a perfectly successful connection. A guess written into a
# runbook is worse than a gap in one, because the next person acts on it. What is
# recorded is the header signature, which is reproducible; what is missing is the
# cause, which needs a dashboard read nobody has done.
#
# WHY A REDIRECT RATHER THAN A PURGE. A purge turns the URL into a 404, which
# breaks every link already sitting in a chat, a reader or a history, and it only
# addresses the tier that stamps cf-cache-status, which measurement says is not the
# tier holding this: the orphan stamped DYNAMIC while an /assets/ file on the same
# zone went MISS then HIT. A 301 needs no theory of the cause to work, and it fixes
# the half a purge cannot, since two URLs serving one article is a duplicate and
# 301 is the instruction that consolidates them.
#
# BOTH PATH FORMS, and this is the part the first version of this file got wrong.
# Pages matches a _redirects source literally. The rule ending in a slash did not
# cover the same URL without one, which kept answering 404 for half a day after the
# fix was called done. Measured, not assumed. Every future entry gets both lines.

